  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253263/assets/7c8c3764dcdd63bb7f7078ccdb729a95712f788db25fc1ba048172fc06b2b3d1.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSo, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253263/assets/75e6fabf01bb0a73e17efee8d9d52d7c1e66cfd78eccd3d5c4d1f9e087335c1f.png)\n\nSince there are $3$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =3\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
